Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Permission Denied Errors

If sysctl -p or ethtool -G/-K/-C fail with permission errors, confirm you are running them with sudo and that the parameter is not marked read-only inside a container (cat /proc/version and check if you are in a container with systemd-detect-virt). Investigate: does the restriction come from the kernel, from a container runtime's seccomp/capability restrictions, or from a cloud provider's virtual NIC driver not exposing that control at all?

Issue 2: ethtool Settings Not Supported / Have No Effect

If ethtool -G or -C silently report "not supported" or the "Current hardware settings" never change after applying them, check ethtool -i $INTERFACE for the driver name (e.g., virtio_net, vmxnet3, ena) and research whether that specific virtual NIC driver implements ring buffer or coalescing configuration at all. Ask yourself: is this a genuine kernel/driver limitation, or did the command run before the interface was fully up?

Issue 3: iperf3 Connection Issues

If the client cannot connect to the server, work through the following diagnostic questions rather than assuming the fix: Is the iperf3 server process actually listening on the expected port (ss -tlnp | grep 5001 on the server)? Is a firewall (firewalld, ufw, or cloud security group rules) blocking port 5001 between the two machines? Does a basic ping $SERVER_IP succeed, ruling out full connectivity loss versus a port-specific block? If the server was started in the foreground and the SSH session closed, is the process still running, or does it need nohup iperf3 -s -p 5001 & / a systemd service to persist?

Conclusion
In this lab, you established a network performance baseline, systematically tuned TCP buffer sizes and NIC-level settings (ring buffers, offloads, and interrupt coalescing) using sysctl and ethtool, and made those changes persistent across reboots with a systemd service. You then measured the effect of each optimization using iperf3, switching from fragile text-scraping to JSON output parsed with jq for reliable, repeatable throughput comparisons. Along the way you learned that the size of a measurable improvement depends heavily on the bandwidth-delay product of the link being tested, and that tuning which looks negligible on a low-latency 1 Gbps LAN can be decisive on higher-latency or higher-bandwidth paths. Next steps for continued tuning include testing across a real WAN link with higher RTT to see the buffer optimizations produce a larger measurable gain, experimenting with alternative congestion control algorithms (cubic, bbr, reno) under packet loss, and integrating the JSON-based comparison script into a scheduled job so throughput regressions can be detected automatically over time.
